WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.278 --> 00:00:05.829
- [Narrator] In my career, I've never met

00:00:05.829 --> 00:00:09.313
a single person with his passion, ever,

00:00:09.313 --> 00:00:11.021
and there's not even a close second.

00:00:11.021 --> 00:00:12.328
- I get bloody.

00:00:12.328 --> 00:00:14.967
I put my body into the
capture of the hunt.

00:00:14.967 --> 00:00:17.510
- The guy will go anywhere,
climb any mountain

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.817
to catch a fish in a stream.

00:00:19.817 --> 00:00:22.867
He's doing basically
the work of a biologist

00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:24.896
just as sort of a volunteer.

00:00:24.896 --> 00:00:27.007
- The science is important
because it's gonna help us

00:00:27.007 --> 00:00:29.080
protect more populations
of these fish that are,

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.003
some are only in one
creek, some are in one river.

00:00:32.003 --> 00:00:34.289
It may be just another trout
but it's a special trout

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:36.789
and they're all special to me.

00:00:42.377 --> 00:00:44.339
- [Narrator] Steve Macmillan,
what's the first impression

00:00:44.339 --> 00:00:45.834
you get when you meet him?

00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:47.582
- Nice. That looks like a perky there.

00:00:47.582 --> 00:00:48.415
- Yeah it does.

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:50.179
- And it's almost difficult to understand

00:00:50.179 --> 00:00:53.225
him on the phone because
he's so excited about fish

00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:55.701
and he talks so fast.

00:00:55.701 --> 00:00:58.388
The one word to sum up Steve is passion.

00:00:58.388 --> 00:01:01.729
- I work in the big city, work long hours.

00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:02.767
I take a lot of work home with me.

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:04.716
I have a lot of stress at work.

00:01:04.716 --> 00:01:07.935
On weekends I find myself
in these hills walking

00:01:07.935 --> 00:01:09.522
long mountain trails to go up there

00:01:09.522 --> 00:01:11.477
and try to catch a fish.

00:01:11.477 --> 00:01:13.149
I feel like I own the place and many times

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:14.732
I do own the place.

00:01:17.134 --> 00:01:19.531
- About four or five years ago was

00:01:19.531 --> 00:01:21.231
the Nevada Native Slam Program,

00:01:21.231 --> 00:01:24.769
a challenge to anglers in Nevada to catch

00:01:24.769 --> 00:01:27.452
every native species, that
includes Lahontan Cutthroat,

00:01:27.452 --> 00:01:29.319
the Bonneville Cutthroat,
Yellowstone Cutthroat,

00:01:29.319 --> 00:01:32.692
Red Band, Bull Trout,
and Mountain Whitefish.

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.725
- [Steve] So I began
trekking the state to catch

00:01:34.725 --> 00:01:36.350
these six species.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:37.489
- It's not easy.

00:01:37.489 --> 00:01:39.849
You need to travel great lengths to catch

00:01:39.849 --> 00:01:42.729
some of these species of
fish and they're in extremely

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:43.992
remote, rugged locations.

00:01:43.992 --> 00:01:46.306
Well, Steve did it in
two and a half months.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:47.852
- For me, to be the first to achieve it,

00:01:47.852 --> 00:01:49.563
it was very humbling.

00:01:49.563 --> 00:01:51.429
The last fish was probably for most people

00:01:51.429 --> 00:01:53.016
the easiest one to catch, but for me

00:01:53.016 --> 00:01:54.647
I drove almost 3,000 miles over five trips

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.234
to catch it.

00:01:56.234 --> 00:01:57.900
When I caught that thing,
it was like winning

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:58.754
the Super Bowl.

00:01:58.754 --> 00:02:00.421
For me, it's basically finding that spot,

00:02:00.421 --> 00:02:02.046
knowing they're there watching, observing,

00:02:02.046 --> 00:02:03.548
instead of just being in a rush.

00:02:03.548 --> 00:02:06.024
I practice a technique I've named CPR.

00:02:06.024 --> 00:02:07.894
Capture, photograph, release.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:08.727
There we go.

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:10.404
And get a good shot for posterity.

00:02:10.404 --> 00:02:13.077
- He documents everything
that happens that day

00:02:13.077 --> 00:02:16.380
and he gets stream
temperature, air temperature,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.960
lengths and weights of
fish and pictures of that

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.710
and he'll prepare a
report and send it to us.

00:02:24.171 --> 00:02:26.810
He'll be out on a stream
that we haven't been to

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.880
in 50, 60 years, and he'll
grab a fin clip for us

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.603
and that fin sample is
used for genetic analysis

00:02:33.603 --> 00:02:37.390
to determine if, you know, that
population is genetically pure

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.534
or hybridized.

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:41.546
He has found species of fish in places

00:02:41.546 --> 00:02:42.968
that we had no idea about.

00:02:42.968 --> 00:02:45.123
So he's doing the work of a biologist

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:47.456
just as sort of a volunteer.

